---
// How it works (D-028): describes what the task pages actually do, using the same constants.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CLOSENESS_LABEL } from '../lib/tasks';
import { EVIDENCE_STATUS_LABEL, TESTED_THRESHOLD, TYPICAL_REQUEST } from '../lib/overview';
import { imported } from '../lib/imported';

const { epoch, modelsdev, lmarena } = imported();
---
<Base title="How it works" description="Where Normie Mode's AI rankings come from, how the task score works, and what we do and don't claim.">
  <article style="max-width: 46rem">
    <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <p class="lede">We rank AI models for everyday tasks using independent tests and people's votes, and show you the evidence under every answer.</p>

    <h2>Where the evidence comes from</h2>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li><strong>Tests of real work</strong>, collected by <a href={epoch?.meta.url}>Epoch AI</a> from the people who run each test ({epoch?.meta.licence}).</li>
      <li><strong>People's votes</strong> from <a href={lmarena?.meta.url}>LMArena</a>, where people compare two anonymous answers and pick the better one ({lmarena?.meta.licence}).</li>
      <li><strong>Prices and specs</strong> from <a href={modelsdev?.meta.url}>models.dev</a> ({modelsdev?.meta.licence}).</li>
      <li><strong>What the makers say</strong> at launch, always labelled as theirs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every chart names its source underneath.</p>

    <h2>We compare exact model versions</h2>
    <p>ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini are apps. Each runs one or more AI models, and those models change every few months. We compare the models. A new version starts with no results, and we never assume it performs like the one before.</p>

    <h2>How close a test is to your task</h2>
    <p>Hardly any test measures one everyday task exactly, so each test on a task page carries a label:</p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li><strong>{CLOSENESS_LABEL.direct}:</strong> the test is this kind of work.</li>
      <li><strong>{CLOSENESS_LABEL.related}:</strong> it tests a skill the task depends on.</li>
      <li><strong>{CLOSENESS_LABEL.general}:</strong> it tests overall ability, used only when nothing closer exists.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When a more specific task (for example vibe coding) has no tests of its own, it uses the tests from its broader task, labelled as related at most.</p>

    <h2>The task score</h2>
    <p>On each test with at least three models, we place each model from 0 (lowest of the models we track) to 100 (highest). A model's task score is its average across the tests it has results for. Only models with results on at least half the tests get a score.</p>
    <p>It shows who leads among the models we track, not how good they are in absolute terms. A score of 0 means last of this group, not useless.</p>

    <h2>Too close to call</h2>
    <p>Test results come with a likely range, shown as a thin line on the bar. When the top two models' ranges overlap, we say they're too close to call rather than naming a winner.</p>

    <h2>How new a model's evidence is</h2>
    <p>Independent testers take one to three weeks to catch up with a launch, so each model shows where its evidence stands:</p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li><strong>{EVIDENCE_STATUS_LABEL['maker-says']}:</strong> no independent results yet.</li>
      <li><strong>{EVIDENCE_STATUS_LABEL.early}:</strong> fewer than {TESTED_THRESHOLD} independent results.</li>
      <li><strong>{EVIDENCE_STATUS_LABEL.tested}:</strong> {TESTED_THRESHOLD} or more, or a place on Epoch AI's combined capability score.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>Cost is the maker's list price for using the model directly, worked out for 1,000 typical requests of about {TYPICAL_REQUEST.words}. Models that think for longer cost more in practice. Chat apps charge a monthly fee instead.</p>

    <h2>Who writes what</h2>
    <p>Norm, our guide, says two kinds of thing. Anything about the data, such as who leads or what the top models cost, is generated from the same numbers as the charts, so it can't disagree with them. His advice is written by a person and only appears once it's been reviewed.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
